<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    DnnMigration.Domain, the innermost layer of the Clean Architecture and the
    foundation of the DnnMigration backend.

    Every other project in the solution depends on this one, directly or
    transitively (Application to Domain; Infrastructure to Domain and
    Application; Api to Application and Infrastructure; the test projects to
    Application and Api), and this project depends on NOTHING. AAP 0.5.2.2
    holds the full reference graph; every arrow points inward only.

    This file is deliberately near-empty, and its emptiness IS the
    specification. The commentary below is the only content it needs, and it
    exists so that a future maintainer who opens an "empty" project file does
    not conclude something is missing and helpfully add it back.

    ===========================================================================
    1. Rule T1: ZERO project references, ZERO package references. Deliberate.
    ===========================================================================
    This project declares no project references and no package references, and
    no bare assembly or framework references either. That is deliberate, it is
    load bearing, and none may ever be added.

    (The MSBuild item types meant here are the project, package, assembly and
    framework reference items. They are named in prose rather than spelled
    literally so that the repository guard which greps this file for those item
    names keeps returning a count of zero.)

    This is a compile-time architectural guarantee, not a style preference
    (AAP 0.4.3, 0.6.1, 0.8.3): because the Domain project cannot see any outer
    layer and cannot see any third-party library, a layering violation is a
    BUILD FAILURE rather than a review finding. Business logic cannot reach a
    controller and data access cannot bypass a repository interface, because
    either would fail to compile. Adding one single dependency here silently
    destroys that guarantee for the entire solution.

    Package ownership is assigned per layer (AAP 0.6.1): Domain owns nothing,
    Application owns FluentValidation, Infrastructure owns EF Core, SqlClient,
    BCrypt and the health checks, and Api owns JWT, Swagger, API versioning and
    Serilog. That split is what keeps this layer free of infrastructure
    concerns as a compile-time fact rather than a convention.

    If a Domain type ever appears to need a package, the design is wrong: move
    the concern outward to Application or Infrastructure. Three concrete
    temptations have already been considered and rejected:

      * Computed, non-persisted properties (for example the bit flag helpers
        over the legacy SupportedFeatures column) are plain read-only C#
        properties here, and are excluded with Ignore() in the Infrastructure
        Fluent configuration. Do NOT reach for [NotMapped]: it would drag in
        EF Core or System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations.Schema.

      * Validation attributes such as Required or MaxLength do NOT belong here.
        Request validation is FluentValidation, in the Application layer
        (AAP 0.4.1.1).

      * Serialisation attributes such as XmlRoot, XmlElement and their JSON
        equivalents do NOT belong here. The legacy Info classes carried them;
        the migration drops them from the domain on purpose (AAP 0.1.2.1).
        DTOs own the wire contract and no entity ever crosses the API boundary
        (AAP 0.8.3).

    ===========================================================================
    2. Build policy is INHERITED. Do not duplicate it here.
    ===========================================================================
    backend/Directory.Build.props is imported implicitly by MSBuild, which walks
    upward from this directory, and it is the single source of build policy for
    all six projects (AAP 0.6.3). It already supplies:

      TargetFramework            net8.0
      LangVersion                12.0
      Nullable                   enable
      ImplicitUsings             enable
      TreatWarningsAsErrors      true
      NoWarn                     $(NoWarn);CS8618;CS1591
      GenerateDocumentationFile  true
      EnforceCodeStyleInBuild    true

    None of those is redeclared here, on purpose: one policy must have exactly
    one home, or the two copies drift apart silently.

    AssemblyName and RootNamespace are absent for the same reason. The SDK
    derives both from the project filename, giving DnnMigration.Domain for
    each, which is already exactly right, and OutputType already defaults to
    Library. Verified with:
      dotnet msbuild -getProperty:AssemblyName -getProperty:RootNamespace
    Source files need no Compile items either, because the SDK globs all .cs
    files automatically; the legacy DotNetNuke.Library.vbproj enumerated every
    file by hand and that mechanism is obsolete.

    ===========================================================================
    3. Only CS8618 and CS1591 may EVER be suppressed.
    ===========================================================================
    Both are already suppressed centrally in Directory.Build.props, with the
    justification recorded alongside them: CS8618 because entities and DTOs are
    materialised by the framework, so the compiler cannot see the assignment,
    and CS1591 because generating the documentation file while treating
    warnings as errors would otherwise fail the build on every undocumented
    public member. No other warning is suppressed anywhere in this solution
    (AAP 0.6.3, 0.8.3).

    Do NOT add a NoWarn element to this file. If this project ever emits a
    warning under Validation Gate 1

      dotnet build -configuration Release -warnaserror

    then FIX THE CODE. Never suppress it here. For the same reason, do not add
    NuGetAudit, NuGetAuditLevel, WarningsNotAsErrors, AnalysisLevel or any
    other analyser knob: those are owned centrally too, and changing them here
    can turn an advisory into a build-breaking error.

    This project inverts the legacy baseline it replaces. The pre 3.5 format
    Library/DotNetNuke.Library.vbproj set TreatWarningsAsErrors to false with
    WarningLevel 1 and an empty NoWarn, and carried no TargetFrameworkVersion
    element at all. It is retained untouched as a read-only reference input
    under the side by side strangler arrangement (AAP 0.1.1, 0.9.5).
  -->

</Project>
